It formats a standalone paginate tag with a pageInfo and a results variable
{% paginate query as pageInfo, pageEntries %}

It formats a standalone paginate tag with only a results variable
{% paginate query as pageEntries %}

It formats a paginate tag whose query is built inline
{% paginate craft.entries().section('blog').limit(10) as pageInfo, pageEntries %}

It does not require a closing tag, so following markup stays at the same level
{% paginate query as pageInfo, pageEntries %}
<div>{{ pageInfo.currentPage }}</div>

It preserves whitespace control operators
{%- paginate query as pageEntries -%}
{%~ paginate query as pageEntries ~%}

It trims whitespace around the markup
{% paginate query as pageEntries %}

It formats a realistic Craft pagination block
{% set query = craft.entries().section('blog').limit(10) %}
{% paginate query as pageInfo, pageEntries %}
{% for entry in pageEntries %}
  <article>
    <h1>{{ entry.title }}</h1>
  </article>
{% endfor %}
<a href="{{ pageInfo.nextUrl }}">Next</a>
